      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Slow coronary flow (SCF) is a special coronary microvascular disorder associated with recurrent chest pain. The pathogenesis of SCF remain unclear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "We sought to assess whether serum salusin-β levels are correlated with SCF."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We enrolled 76 patients with angiographically confirmed SCF and 108 age- and gender-matched controls. We measured serum salusin-β levels by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay and coronary flow rate was assessed using thrombolysis in myocardial infarction frame count (TFC)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Serum salusin-β levels were elevated in SCF patients compared with controls (4.33 [range 3.52-5.87] nmol/L vs. 3.76 [range 2.98-4.67] nmol/L). Multivariate logistic regression analysis revealed that salusin-β in serum was the independent predictor of SCF (odds ratio\u2009=\u20091.814). Serum salusin-β levels were independently correlated with mean-TFC (r\u2009=\u20090.355, p\u2009=\u20090.002)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Serum salusin-β levels were independently associated with SCF. Therefore, our findings implicate a potential role of salusin-β in the pathophysiology of SCF and provide insights on both risk stratification and modification in this patient population."
        }
      ]},
